findings.body:
The new assertion only passes if the log message matches both the old and the new regex. The two patterns are different sentences ("Refused to frame ... because an ancestor violates" versus "Framing ... violates"), so no message can match both. The error message and the PR intent ("match one of the CSP error patterns") both say it should be `||`.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="105-111"}

::callout{type="warning"}
With `&&`, `NoneAncestorWebSocketCompressionTests` and `NoneAncestorWebSocketAppliesPolicyOnCallbackCompressionTests` will fail on every browser version. They are not just flaky.

Fix:

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}
        Assert.True(
            ParseErrorMessageRegexOld.IsMatch(logs[0].Message) ||
            ParseErrorMessageRegexNew.IsMatch(logs[0].Message),
            $"Expected log message to match one of the CSP error patterns: {ParseErrorMessageRegexOld} or {ParseErrorMessageRegexNew}. Actual: {logs[0].Message}");

RedisEndToEnd.cs quarantines two theories, swaps the dispose order, unseals `WebSocketWrapper`, and moves the `_receiveTcs` reset above the zero-byte check. None of this relates to the Arcade update.

The TCS move changes behaviour: a zero-byte read now consumes the pending result and resets the TCS, so the following real read waits for a new `SetReceiveResult`. That may be intended, but it deserves a rationale in the PR. Unsealing the class has no visible reason, since nothing derives from it.

